# Fictional EP Tracklist

## Track 1: “String Four Arrives Before the Chord”

**Story & Context:** At 112 BPM, Klara holds a minor-seventh grip across six strings but reveals only strings six, four, and three during the opening sixteen bars. Marcus programs a compact boogie kick/snare pattern and allows the electric bass to use only root and fifth, withholding the seventh because its string has not yet appeared. Élodie answers every second transfer with one dry Rhodes dyad. The first Transfer Reset occurs just before the upper chord tone should arrive; Klara returns to string six and repeats the path, turning the missing tone into the hook. Only in the final third does she complete the route with strings two and one. Nico enters immediately afterward with one muted bass-trumpet fifth, marking the first Full-Grip Flash without adding a new chord.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Large tram route sheet with six horizontal lines and one red arrow stopping before the top line, arpeggione frets overlaid like station marks, graphite notation beside a ticket punch. Sparse vocal fragments limited to “string four,” “not the whole chord,” “reset here,” and “top line later.”

## Track 2: “Timetable Keeps the Seventh Hidden”

**Story & Context:** At 116 BPM, Marcus uses the EP’s most active bass groove, but every phrase is forced to avoid the minor seventh until Klara bows the corresponding upper-middle string. The arpeggione alternates OUTER → INNER transfer paths, producing large register jumps without changing fingering. Élodie plays clipped sixth and suspended-second Rhodes shells on offbeats. Midway through, Klara performs three Transfer Resets in succession, and the bass repeatedly approaches the withheld seventh chromatically but refuses to land. When the seventh finally appears, the drums simplify to kick, snare, and one hat line while Marcus plays the previously forbidden bass note for only a single beat. The effect is harmonic release through disclosure rather than progression.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Municipal timetable with the seventh row blacked out until one late-night departure, six string lines crossing a grid of clock times, red correction pencil beside a muted electric-bass pick. Sparse fragments limited to “seventh hidden,” “not on the table,” “one late entry,” and “now disclose.”

## Track 3: “All Six Lines Cross at Meridian”

**Story & Context:** The closer runs at 106 BPM and begins with the sparsest transfer on the EP. Klara holds a bright sixth/add9 voicing but bows only one string per two bars. Marcus removes hats completely and uses one short bass answer after each newly exposed tone. Élodie remains silent for the first minute, allowing the chord to assemble almost entirely through arpeggione register. Nico enters with breath-heavy pedal notes only after the fourth string becomes audible. In the final section, Klara performs a fast LOW → HIGH Full-Grip Flash across all six strings while Marcus reduces the bass to one root pulse and Élodie strikes a single dry Rhodes chord after the bow has finished. The last gesture is the reverse transfer HIGH → LOW, stopping one string early. The unresolved lowest tone is left unheard as room noise closes the EP.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Six tram lines converging at one drafting-point labeled MERIDIAN, ruler and compass marks around the intersection, bowed string arcs crossing a black timetable grid. Sparse vocal fragments limited to “six lines,” “one meridian,” “show them all,” and “leave the last below.”

